          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"“But of course, **I love you**!” he would reply.\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"The speaker is Rodolphe Boulanger, replying to Emma Bovary during their adulterous relationship. Emma repeatedly seeks assurances that he loves her, while Rodolphe has become accustomed to the affair and is less emotionally invested than she is. The imperfect “répondait-il” presents this as a recurring kind of exchange rather than necessarily a unique declaration. Emma treats romantic language as evidence of the consuming passion she desires, whereas Rodolphe’s conduct and the novel’s broader narration make his sincerity doubtful. That disparity matters because Emma increasingly stakes her emotional future on the relationship and will later press for escape from her marriage.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The wording presents love as something Rodolphe affirms to be true: “oui” directly answers Emma’s doubt, and “je t’aime” supplies the proposition being confirmed. Although the reply may reassure her and sustain their affair, that interpersonal consequence does not make the words themselves a promise or undertaking. Likewise, the exclamation mark and “Mais oui” can convey emphasis, impatience, or practiced reassurance without making the utterance a reflexive outburst; emotional intensity is not the same as expressive force. The iterative “he would reply” is especially revealing: the direct quotation remains an assertion, but the narrator frames it as a repeated formula. Rodolphe may be insincere or merely complacent, yet possible deception concerns whether he believes the claim, not its core semantic function as an avowal of feeling.\"}"
